"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,229\nThe Learning Brain. Series 3: Generative AI\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,351\nEpisode 3\n\n3\n00:00:07,431 --> 00:00:09,600\nThe jagged frontier\n\n4\n00:00:12,400 --> 00:00:17,528\nSeven hundred and fifty eight consultants at a global firm were given realistic tasks,\n\n5\n00:00:17,803 --> 00:00:20,359\nsome with an AI model and some without.\n\n6\n00:00:21,052 --> 00:00:23,754\nOn tasks inside the AI's abilities,\n\n7\n00:00:24,020 --> 00:00:27,276\nthose with AI finished twelve percent more tasks,\n\n8\n00:00:27,467 --> 00:00:31,363\na quarter faster, at more than forty percent higher quality.\n\n9\n00:00:31,533 --> 00:00:35,907\nBut one task was chosen to sit just outside what the AI could do.\n\n10\n00:00:36,455 --> 00:00:42,810\nThere, consultants using AI were nineteen percentage points less likely to get the right answer.\n\n11\n00:00:43,292 --> 00:00:45,567\nThe tool sounded just as confident.\n\n12\n00:00:45,737 --> 00:00:48,457\nThe researchers called this a jagged frontier.\n\n13\n00:00:48,923 --> 00:00:52,368\nTasks that look equally hard can fall on either side,\n\n14\n00:00:52,675 --> 00:00:55,420\nand the line is invisible from the inside.\n\n15\n00:00:55,590 --> 00:00:57,467\nAI also levels skill.\n\n16\n00:00:57,946 --> 00:01:01,612\nConsultants below average improved by forty three percent,\n\n17\n00:01:01,884 --> 00:01:03,640\nthose above by seventeen.\n\n18\n00:01:04,208 --> 00:01:07,345\nIn a study of over five thousand support agents,\n\n19\n00:01:07,650 --> 00:01:11,405\nAI raised productivity by fourteen percent overall,\n\n20\n00:01:11,679 --> 00:01:13,943\nand thirty four percent for novices,\n\n21\n00:01:14,169 --> 00:01:16,114\nwith little effect for experts.\n\n22\n00:01:16,284 --> 00:01:19,146\nFor learning, that levelling cuts two ways.\n\n23\n00:01:19,495 --> 00:01:21,645\nNovices gain the most performance,\n\n24\n00:01:21,847 --> 00:01:25,483\nand they are also the least able to tell when the AI is wrong.\n\n25\n00:01:25,653 --> 00:01:28,150\nSuccessful users worked in two ways.\n\n26\n00:01:28,462 --> 00:01:30,109\nCentaurs split the task,\n\n27\n00:01:30,248 --> 00:01:31,751\ndeciding what to hand off.\n\n28\n00:01:32,217 --> 00:01:34,973\nCyborgs worked in constant back and forth.\n\n29\n00:01:35,231 --> 00:01:37,243\nBoth kept judging the output.\n\n30\n00:01:37,413 --> 00:01:38,211\nA caution.\n\n31\n00:01:38,612 --> 00:01:41,122\nThese are workplace studies of performance,\n\n32\n00:01:41,142 --> 00:01:44,236\nnot learning, using models from twenty twenty three.\n\n33\n00:01:44,729 --> 00:01:46,670\nThe frontier has moved since,\n\n34\n00:01:46,747 --> 00:01:48,203\nand it is still jagged.\n\n35\n00:01:48,373 --> 00:01:49,128\nFor teaching.\n\n36\n00:01:49,558 --> 00:01:53,667\nMap your own assignments against the frontier: where does AI help,\n\n37\n00:01:53,816 --> 00:01:55,149\nand where does it mislead?\n\n38\n00:01:55,644 --> 00:01:58,544\nGive students one task deliberately outside it,\n\n39\n00:01:58,603 --> 00:02:00,579\nso they learn to spot the edge.\n\n40\n00:02:00,905 --> 00:02:05,032\nAnd require a check step before any AI answer is accepted.\n\n41\n00:02:05,202 --> 00:02:06,653\nThe frontier is real,\n\n42\n00:02:06,673 --> 00:02:07,782\nbut invisible.\n\n43\n00:02:08,221 --> 00:02:11,847\nTeaching students to find its edge is now part of the job.\n\n"
